WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.967 --> 00:00:02.068
-Welcome back to "Late Night."

00:00:02.068 --> 00:00:03.169
We're here with
Senator Elizabeth Warren.

00:00:03.169 --> 00:00:05.238
You are a former Harvard law
professor.

00:00:05.238 --> 00:00:09.309
How would you grade the legal
argument that was presented

00:00:09.309 --> 00:00:10.977
for overturning Roe v. Wade?

00:00:10.977 --> 00:00:12.579
-Oh, bad.
-Yeah.

00:00:12.579 --> 00:00:13.812
[ Laughter ]

00:00:13.812 --> 00:00:16.282
-But, you know, I want to say
bad not just --

00:00:16.282 --> 00:00:18.284
-Real quick before we go on,
were you a tough grader?

00:00:18.284 --> 00:00:19.952
-Yeah.
-Yeah? Okay.

00:00:19.952 --> 00:00:21.287
-Yeah.
[ Laughter ]

00:00:21.287 --> 00:00:23.089
-So just know if she gives it
an F, Clarence Thomas,

00:00:23.089 --> 00:00:25.591
it's probably a D
from somebody else.

00:00:25.591 --> 00:00:27.193
-But it's still a D.
-Okay, yeah.

00:00:27.193 --> 00:00:28.628
-It's still bad.

00:00:28.628 --> 00:00:31.498
Here's what really -- one of the
things that really troubles me

00:00:31.498 --> 00:00:33.866
about the way
this opinion was written.

00:00:33.866 --> 00:00:36.536
You know, when you're out there
to change something

00:00:36.536 --> 00:00:40.539
that has been precedent for half
a century, you would expect to

00:00:40.539 --> 00:00:44.910
read an opinion that really is
as grounded in law as you could

00:00:44.910 --> 00:00:47.747
possibly make it, that talks
about the restrictions,

00:00:47.747 --> 00:00:49.315
what you can't do,

00:00:49.315 --> 00:00:52.986
and ultimately really
tries to persuade.

00:00:52.986 --> 00:00:55.188
This opinion doesn't
do any of that.

00:00:55.188 --> 00:00:59.158
This opinion basically cites
a bunch of folks from the 1700s

00:00:59.158 --> 00:01:02.094
and says, "Boy, that's the way
we want to go."

00:01:02.094 --> 00:01:06.232
Oh, I'm sorry.
To a time when aristocrats ran

00:01:06.232 --> 00:01:10.903
the world, when the only people
who had voices were white men,

00:01:10.903 --> 00:01:16.209
and when slavery was a way
for people to make money.

00:01:16.209 --> 00:01:20.012
That's not the America we're
trying to build going forward.

00:01:20.012 --> 00:01:26.286
And the fact that this opinion
was just such an in-your-face

00:01:26.286 --> 00:01:33.893
about people who are truly
going to be hurt from it,

00:01:33.893 --> 00:01:37.497
people whose lives are going to
be turned upside down,

00:01:37.497 --> 00:01:42.502
people who are poor,
who are not going to have

00:01:42.502 --> 00:01:45.271
the opportunity to leave and get
to another state,

00:01:45.271 --> 00:01:47.807
women of color who already have

00:01:47.807 --> 00:01:50.843
the highest maternal
mortality rates,

00:01:50.843 --> 00:01:57.216
14-year-olds who have been raped
by their coaches,

00:01:57.216 --> 00:02:01.854
mamas who are already working
three jobs to try

00:02:01.854 --> 00:02:05.791
to take care of the families
they've got.

00:02:05.791 --> 00:02:12.465
This opinion had nothing about
the human impact of what

00:02:12.465 --> 00:02:18.437
it means to take away
the decision that a woman

00:02:18.437 --> 00:02:22.775
makes about continuing
a pregnancy and to insert

00:02:22.775 --> 00:02:26.546
instead the government
just to come in callously

00:02:26.546 --> 00:02:32.085
and makes the decision instead
of the person who is pregnant.

00:02:32.085 --> 00:02:37.723
It troubles me to think how
six people could say they have

00:02:37.723 --> 00:02:41.227
seized power and care so little

00:02:41.227 --> 00:02:42.929
about the people
who will pay the price.

00:02:42.929 --> 00:02:45.898
-You know, there's been a lot of
talk about the steps you could

00:02:45.898 --> 00:02:48.767
take to maybe change
the direction of the court,

00:02:48.767 --> 00:02:51.571
things like stacking the court,
things like term limits.

00:02:51.571 --> 00:02:53.640
We talked about
abolishing the filibuster.

00:02:53.640 --> 00:02:56.108
These are things that
when they're brought up,

00:02:56.108 --> 00:03:00.280
oftentimes people categorize
as extremist positions.

00:03:00.280 --> 00:03:02.081
I certainly understand
they sound extreme.

00:03:02.081 --> 00:03:04.584
Do you think that as people
watch the court

00:03:04.584 --> 00:03:07.887
take such extreme steps and make
decisions that roll back

00:03:07.887 --> 00:03:10.990
precedent 50 years,
do you think those extreme steps

00:03:10.990 --> 00:03:12.792
seem less extreme to people now?

00:03:12.792 --> 00:03:16.596
-I think that that what we're
watching is how many more people

00:03:16.596 --> 00:03:22.534
see this as an extremist court
and see the kind of conditions

00:03:22.534 --> 00:03:30.175
that this tiny group of radicals
are trying to impose on

00:03:30.175 --> 00:03:34.213
the rest of the country
and the kind of threat they pose

00:03:34.213 --> 00:03:37.383
to all of us with a decision
on guns,

00:03:37.383 --> 00:03:41.553
with a decision on abortion,
with a decision on climate.

00:03:41.553 --> 00:03:44.857
Now, I think that means
the first thing we have to do is

00:03:44.857 --> 00:03:47.327
remind ourselves
the Supreme Court

00:03:47.327 --> 00:03:49.996
doesn't get the final word.

00:03:49.996 --> 00:03:53.666
The final word rests with we,
the people.

00:03:53.666 --> 00:03:58.838
And that's why it's so important
at this moment to be angry

00:03:58.838 --> 00:04:02.675
but to be focused in on
that election in November.

00:04:02.675 --> 00:04:05.778
Two senators hang on
to the house.

00:04:05.778 --> 00:04:07.713
-You're making it very easy
to follow.

00:04:07.713 --> 00:04:09.581
-And we've got this.

00:04:09.581 --> 00:04:14.520
But it's so important because,
Seth, understand this,

00:04:14.520 --> 00:04:18.925
if we don't do that, they've
already told us the direction

00:04:18.925 --> 00:04:19.926
they're headed in.

00:04:19.926 --> 00:04:21.560
They're not stopping at Roe.

00:04:21.560 --> 00:04:24.029
You didn't hear them at the end
of this say,

00:04:24.029 --> 00:04:26.432
"Oh, we got Roe, the climate,
and guns. We're done."

00:04:26.432 --> 00:04:28.834
No.
Clarence Thomas already said,

00:04:28.834 --> 00:04:31.303
"We're coming after
the LGBTQ community.

00:04:31.303 --> 00:04:34.540
We are coming after
contraception.

00:04:34.540 --> 00:04:38.677
We are coming after what people
do in the privacy

00:04:38.677 --> 00:04:44.050
of their own homes and their
intimate and family lives."

00:04:44.050 --> 00:04:48.287
They are coming after the things
we hold dear.

00:04:48.287 --> 00:04:51.523
And when you connect that up
with what's happened

00:04:51.523 --> 00:04:54.660
with the January 6th
insurrection,

00:04:54.660 --> 00:05:00.466
it's not an exaggeration to say
our democracy is truly at stake.

00:05:00.466 --> 00:05:05.138
If the Republicans can seize
power, they have made it clear

00:05:05.138 --> 00:05:07.640
that they stand
behind Donald Trump,

00:05:07.640 --> 00:05:11.043
who wanted to be a dictator,

00:05:11.043 --> 00:05:15.847
tried really hard to get there
and got really close.

00:05:15.847 --> 00:05:19.018
What scares me is next time
around they're going to

00:05:19.018 --> 00:05:20.920
figure out how to
succeed at that.

00:05:20.920 --> 00:05:23.890
And that's why what we do
in November is so important.

00:05:23.890 --> 00:05:26.425
-I want to try to end on
a little bit more optimism.

00:05:26.425 --> 00:05:27.893
We've got the 4th of July
coming.

00:05:27.893 --> 00:05:30.263
Are you at least going to give
yourself a break and barbecue?

00:05:30.263 --> 00:05:33.733
-You know, a little barbecue,
a little hiking.

00:05:33.733 --> 00:05:35.034
-Oh, wow.

00:05:35.034 --> 00:05:38.136
-But you got to be back
in the fight the next day.

00:05:38.136 --> 00:05:39.204
-Yeah.
-Sorry.

00:05:39.204 --> 00:05:40.807
That's how everything turns,
right?

00:05:40.807 --> 00:05:43.008
-Seems like 24 hours is about
all the time

00:05:43.008 --> 00:05:44.543
you'll give anybody to do
anything. Yeah.

00:05:44.543 --> 00:05:46.245
-Yeah, that's --
-That's not fighting.

00:05:46.245 --> 00:05:47.880
[ Laughter ]

00:05:47.880 --> 00:05:49.615
Hey, thank you so much
for being here, Senator.

00:05:49.615 --> 00:05:50.750
It's always a pleasure.
-So good to see you.

00:05:50.750 --> 00:05:52.218
-Senator Elizabeth Warren,
everybody.

00:05:52.218 --> 00:05:54.087
We'll be right back
with Cazzie David.

